  "id": "DrugBank_DB14568 - MONDO_grouped_18874_4643",
  "verbalization": "Ivosidenib is associated with Myeloid Leukemia through a mechanism involving its use in treating a subset of acute myeloid leukemia (AML) cases, including Therapy Related Acute Myeloid Leukemia And Myelodysplastic Syndrome, Inherited Acute Myeloid Leukemia, and Unclassified Acute Myeloid Leukemia. These conditions are all subtypes of AML that share a common pathogenic pathway involving the use of Mitoxantrone, a chemotherapy agent that is known to induce or exacerbate AML in certain patient populations. The evidence suggests that Ivosidenib may exert its therapeutic effect by targeting pathways that are dysregulated in these leukemia subtypes, potentially through mechanisms involving DNA repair or cell cycle regulation. The overlapping indications and the shared association with Mitoxantrone imply a broader role for Ivosidenib in the treatment of AML, particularly in cases where Mitoxantrone is used as part of a therapeutic regimen. The integration of these pathways highlights the potential for Ivosidenib to be a valuable addition to the treatment of Myeloid Leukemia, especially in patients with specific genetic or clinical profiles.",
